Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:06.039
OK, let's take a break from the world of lies
coming to us from the White House about the

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:07.160
virus.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.160
Let's look at science.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.389
All right.

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:15.000
If you've been watching our show, you would
already know about a bunch of possible treatments,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.580
not game changers, the way Trump is saying.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.180
We're talking about possible corona virus
treatments.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.789
The question has mostly been, are these safe
enough to use in proper clinical trials?

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:30.689
And when might that start?

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:37.140
The World Health Organization is starting
an unprecedented quadruple clinical trial

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.489
where doctors around the world can participate.

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:41.840
This is called solidarity.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.760
I don't know if that is an acronym for something.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.760
It may be.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:46.760
Maybe, maybe not.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.609
This is basically taking what would often
be a very long process and compressing it

00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:54.219
into simultanous trials.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:55.720
They are not blinded trials.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.470
We'll talk about that in a second.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.750
And four different treatments are going to
be tried because we have so many patients.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:07.840
You can try four different things at once
compared to placebo and the standard treatment.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.660
In this case, they may not be using placebo.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.720
That may be unethical given the circumstances.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.180
Thousands of patients across multiple countries
are going to be included.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.880
Now, this is being done both to consider these
drugs as treatments for people who are already

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.460
sick, but also with the possibility that maybe
some of these could become prophylactics in

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.740
advance of a vaccine to protect people from
the virus.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:31.740
We don't know.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.740
That's what's being tested.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.740
All right.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.290
The four drugs being tested would be familiar
to anyone who's been watching our show since

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:40.970
I told you about these weeks ago.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.800
We've got the anti-viral REM desert here.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.800
That's from Gilli.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.030
AD Farma developed for Ebola.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:53.250
Didn't work well for Ebola going to be tested
in this large W.H.O. clinical trial.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:58.630
Number two already spoke about it, chloroquine
or hydroxy chloroquine, another form of the

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:03.450
same drug around since 1934, used to treat
malaria.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:09.590
Side effects can include muscle problems,
loss of appetite, diarrhea and skin rash.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:16.700
Serious side effects can include vision issues,
muscle damage, seizures, low blood cell levels,

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.410
which itself can lead to subsequent problems.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.220
Small test showed it worked on a small sample
of coronavirus patients.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.130
It was not blinded and it was not randomized.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:33.600
Now there will be more testing done in combination
with a zero Meissen.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.680
Number three, a combination of two HIV drugs,
low pin of there and Raton of air.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.810
That's the third trial that's being done.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.100
And then lastly, the fourth test will be those
two HIV drugs.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.100
Le Pen.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.550
Le Pen here and return of air.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:54.640
With the addition of interfer on beta, doctors
will be able to put their patients information

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:01.850
into a W.H.O. computer system, see who's eligible
for which test account for underlying conditions,

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:06.660
which may make someone not a good candidate
for one test, but may be an OK candidate for

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.660
another.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.180
This is a man massive major undertaking.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.840
This is the large trial that we've been waiting
for, which starts this week.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.530
Now, of course, looking at already approved
drugs, drugs approved for other things is

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:26.810
going to be the fastest path, much faster
than developing new drugs for sure.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:32.130
But then in addition, they're also looking
at adding trials of drugs that have not yet

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:38.190
been approved, but that have worked well in
animal trials on other types of respiratory

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.190
viruses.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.970
Meurs, SaaS, et cetera.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.550
So as I mentioned, again, these are not double
blind tests.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.590
Double-blind would be neither the doctor nor
the patient knows who's getting which treatment.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.720
The idea is you prevent a placebo.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.349
You prevent the doctor from consciously or
subconsciously giving the trial treatment

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:58.150
to patients that may be predisposed to do
better.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.700
Any way that would skew the data.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.920
But still, this is the way it's being done.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.870
A number of medical ethicists, including our
friend from NYU, Arthur Caplan, say given

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:11.390
the circumstances, this this is a good test.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:16.790
So, as always, none of these things will give
us answers as soon as we would like.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:21.070
The sooner you start, the sooner you get data,
the sooner you can start to figure out whether

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:24.350
one of these may may be a good path forward.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.710
Sometimes when you do this, you will very
quickly see one of these four treatments is

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.680
significantly better than the standard treatment
and significantly better than the other treatments

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.190
that are being tested.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:40.980
When the results are overwhelming in these
emergency situations, you will stop sometimes

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.320
see the case made that you need to drastically
expand giving patients the treatment that

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.210
appears to be working.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.570
We just don't know yet.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:55.140
Then you have to start dealing with supply
and distribution issues, all sorts of other

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.340
things.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.340
Those would be figured out.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.780
So that's the big trial going on.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.620
Unprecedented quadruple clinical trial.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.960
We'll see what comes from it.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.639
Let's have realistic expectations.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:07.639
Make sure.

